{
  "dom_id": "step-q108281",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/b39c4e174ccb11a533586b57f26958a8ebfb5de0f0920a86297653471862fa7f.png)\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the function shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-3$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=0$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=3$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-3.$ For example, in the interval $(-3.5,-2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/be3b86690beb961d54380b72736d945af3d735410da3bfe34cfb2cdead859f81.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(-3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-3.5,-2.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=0.$ For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/439117bd94dbc147d3cd2de49abd45c4b4790251ce25d1a79ecb31e03081068c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(0)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-0.5,0.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.75,3.25),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227500/assets/33aee4724507cff3e9ca87c78aa77c0ecb0df5f59a7bcf77359c1ca4e957f73c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(3)\\le f(x)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.75,3.25).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
